WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.020
Pinbot dares you to survive.

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:27.220
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.260
I'm Mike.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:29.480
I'm Sean.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.560
And I'm Joe. Guys, Pinbot is a conversion of the pinball machine by the same name.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.060
I don't know if you guys have ever seen a pinball machine in the flesh.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.500
I'm sure we've talked about that in another episode.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:51.460
But the pinbot machine, you know, maybe one of the more well-known pinball machines, if that's even a thing in this day and age.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.640
I've never heard of this one.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:01:05.780
I mean, I remember seeing, like, in more recent video game versions of pinball machines, like, you know, the ones on the PS3 and that kind of era.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.440
I've seen like, you know, the magic one and the whatever.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.020
But I've never heard of pinbot.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:12.000
Theater of magic.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.080
Yeah, theater of magic.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:19.480
That one. Yeah, I know very little about pinball and pinbot is no exception.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.880
I've never heard. I never until right now knew that was a real machine.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.780
Right. And so that brings up a good question before we get into this game, because Sean, you were having me think there for a second.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:34.460
Like, you know, Theater of Magic and Tales of the Arabian Nights because we both played pinball arcade on the PlayStation 3.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.620
I basically forced you to play it.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:38.140
You did force me, yes.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:39.460
You know, that's why you know those games.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:42.580
But those aren't even like some of the most well-known pinball machines.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:44.320
They're well-known by pinball fanatics.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.700
But like, what is a well-known pinball machine?

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:49.420
Like, even for arcades, right?

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.000
Like, most people know Pong, Pac-Man, Donkey Kong.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:56.180
But could you name a pinball machine that everybody knows?

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.620
There's gotta be like a Simpsons one that's everywhere.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:01.540
There is. Simpsons pinball party.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.940
I feel like that's gotta be up there.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.740
I'm sure there's a Star Wars or Star Trek.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:09.980
I think the Star Trek one's probably pretty popular.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:18.600
Those sound like, you know, the entry level, oh, I'm trying to get into pinball machines.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:26.580
There was somewhere in my hometown, I don't remember where, where there was always a pinball machine that was an X-Files themed pinball machine.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:29.740
I just remember it was always playing the X-Files music.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:37.280
That tracks. So that's my experience with pinball is an unknown area where there was an X-Files.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:38.820
It's kind of actually very X-Files-y.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.640
It's like a lost, forgotten area of my life where there was an X-Files themed playing pinball machine.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:52.280
And it's funny because those are pinball machines that probably feel comfortable to play because you're familiar with the IP.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.560
And that is definitely what pinball machines now have become.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:01.020
There have been very expensive, like, pinball Avengers and pinball Wizard of Oz.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:11.820
And I'm not joking. Those are two very recent examples of, like, very decked out, super modern pinball machines that have, like, amazing features that pinball machines just couldn't do until recently.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:23.220
And it's like, there's not really, to go back to, like, the Pac-Man example, there isn't a pinball of Pac-Man where, like, it's just every game is exactly the same.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:28.640
So it's more like pinball as a concept, not necessarily, like, one pinball machine that everybody knows.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:35.160
And so it's kind of weird to have, like, pinbot be translated to video game form, right?

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:45.040
Like, to pick this one and be like, all right, like, everybody loves this pinball machine so much that we should make a video game version of it where you can just play it all the time.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:04:01.220
I mean, even though I can't speak to whether that's true or not, I do prefer this to the, I guess, like, made-for-TV movie versions of pinball machines that we've seen previously.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:08.680
Um, because the things that I associate with a good pinball machine are actually here now.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:22.660
Like, in the previous ones, like, you know, just pinball or rockin' ball or whatever, it was generic to a fault and everything was just bumpers and it was, like, weirdly symmetrical.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:30.540
And there was none of that, like, stuff that just felt, I guess the word would be, it wasn't, like, satisfying to watch.

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:37.160
Like, you know, if you get a, if you're playing an actual pinball machine, there's those things that are just satisfying.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:46.020
Um, this one actually has, like, areas that have functions that are more than just, like, hit this stuff for points.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:52.100
And there's the tracks that go into loops and, like, that really is more of a physical thing.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:58.760
And you're, like, oh, it feels good to get that, to get that pinball up in that track and that, you know what I'm talking about, right?

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.500
Yeah, it feels very analog.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.780
Yes. Oddly, even though it's obviously all, you know, all digital.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:16.660
But probably because it was designed first as, you know, by people who actually design pinball machines in an actual physical space.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:24.540
Not to say that I know that, you know, the people who made rockin' ball or pinball for the NES weren't those same people.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:31.200
But it just feels like it wasn't worried about being a video game when it was first designed and then it was translated to a video game.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:36.400
So then either way, you still have, like, a good pinball machine because you weren't trying to do both at the same time.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:57.620
Now, PinBot has a really cool motif behind it in both the gameplay sense and in the idea of, like, what it looks like as a pinball machine where, you know, in a loose way, the pinball machine itself is this, like, robot that you are playing against that talks to you.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:02.860
And as you complete things, like, it says things back to you and tells you what to do.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.260
That feels very alive and fresh and fresh for the pinball genre, too.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.880
Like, that wasn't something that was being done before PinBot.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:19.000
You had just, like, the, you know, the scoreboard with your current score and if you got, like, a multiball or something, like, it would say it on there.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:24.880
But to have, like, the idea of the machine itself be alive is a pretty cool idea.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:28.160
So how well did that translate into the video form?

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.960
I think it worked very well.

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:38.320
Are you saying that this is the first pinball machine, like, physical pinball machine with, like, missions?

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:44.000
Not missions, but, like, with actual, like, it's trying to tell some kind of pinball story?

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:51.840
No, no, no. I'm saying it itself, like, the machine is, like, a, you know, like, it's supposed to be the character.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:53.000
Yeah. Right. I see.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.460
Okay. I mean, I think that this was pulled off pretty well here.

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:07.580
I also just like, because this isn't a thing that we really saw in the previous pinball games, that it did have those, like, achievements, I guess, that you're trying to do.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:17.800
And adding that with the personality that I've come to associate with, like, good pinball machines, I appreciate that that was there.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:19.660
And it was, you know, it talked to you.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:26.920
And, you know, it's not often that we see actual recorded lines playing on the NES.

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:29.020
So that gave it some authenticity, too.

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:29.920
I liked it.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:31.900
Yeah, I appreciated it.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:36.180
But I'll be honest, I most of the time didn't know what it was trying to say to me.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:39.820
That's because you couldn't understand the speech, Joe?

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:50.740
Yes. Yeah. And to be fair, you know, it's pretty cool always to have, and it's not the first example in the NES, but to have some kind of synthesizer speech form for a character.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:58.960
But the weird thing isn't how it's saying those lines or, you know, the delivery from a voiceover standpoint.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.900
It's that the gameplay itself is interrupted while the speech is happening.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.660
It's like it couldn't handle doing both.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:10.940
And I feel like that's where it loses me a bit.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:20.000
That obviously in a real pinball arcade cabinet, it wouldn't, like, freeze your ball in location and wait for Pinbot to finish what it was saying.

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:23.900
It would just continue on and you would have to, like, listen in and be engaged.

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:28.280
Whereas here it's like your ball freezes wherever it may be.

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:31.500
And now you're just, you know, talking to Pinbot.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:44.940
That's funny because I guess it didn't really, I didn't really feel that way only because it makes sense that there'd be like a dramatic pause during a line in a video game sense.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:53.160
So it didn't feel as confusing that, like, oh, this is breaking my simulation of a real pinball machine.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:09:01.140
But now I totally understand that if you're familiar with the machine, that would not really work out for you.

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:17.960
Yeah, but in video game form, though, there is a perk there, too, where because it pauses, like, you might have paused it in a great location to, like, take a pause for a second and be able to then think about, like, where you're going to lock for the flipper.

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:24.600
Like, is this thing going straight down into the gutter or am I going to be able to, you know, scoop it back up?

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:39.300
There is one thing, though, more so than the synthesized speech, more so than the translation from pinball machine to video game, that is a questionable design decision that I feel like we need to get out of the way.

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:43.920
And that is something that no other pinball game thus far has had to do.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:46.000
So it is a choice.

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:47.720
The split screen?

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:49.260
And that is the split screen.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:10:12.480
They have made it so that at any time, no matter where your ball is on the, let's be honest, it's not a giant screen, but, like, wherever it is on that screen, you will always see the bottom half of your split where your flippers are travels up with you and then eventually just seamlessly connects back to where it actually is on the board.

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:15.500
And it definitely takes some getting used to.

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:18.740
You can calibrate for it.

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:30.340
But overall, I just found it to be unnecessary, especially because it's not, the ball isn't going so fast that it's surprising to be like, oh, and now there's my flippers.

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:32.820
Like, I just, it wasn't that zoomed in on the ball.

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:34.400
The ball wasn't moving that fast.

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:38.820
I never felt like I needed to be anticipating it, like, at that speed.

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:45.040
So the split screen between the flippers and the rest of the action just felt unnecessary.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:46.360
And a little jarring.

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:48.100
I'll agree. Yeah.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:55.080
At first, I thought that the split screen, well, like, initially, I was like, well, what is actually the point of this?

00:10:55.140 --> 00:11:01.820
Like, you know, it's cool to be able to see it, but I'm not going to need to know where, like, I know where the flippers are.

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:05.420
And I'm not going to need to see them until the ball gets close to them.

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:08.560
So it felt like it was taking up a little bit of the screen.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:13.100
And then when it introduced a second ball, I was like, okay, this makes sense.

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:18.700
So I'll be able to kind of, like, watch my flippers if a ball goes down towards there and also watch the other ball.

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:22.860
But when the second ball is on the screen, the camera doesn't follow it up.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:24.180
Like, you're still on the flippers.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:28.780
So I don't know what the purpose of the split screen is at any point.

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:33.460
I mean, I agree that it was definitely jarring at first.

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:42.780
When there is a multi-ball, it will track the ball, but it'll only track the one that's closest to the bottom of the screen.

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:50.680
So maybe you just always had one that was lurking near the flippers so then it never tracked back up.

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:55.780
Well, but even so, if it only tracks the one that's closest to the bottom of the screen, it's still unnecessary.

00:11:56.280 --> 00:12:02.540
Well, then you never get to see the top of the machine if they did that during regular play.

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:04.340
And there's stuff up there.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:12.820
Yeah, but if you're not tracking the one that's further away from the flippers, then that could still happen anyways.

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:20.840
And if you're only tracking the one that's close to the flippers, then you would still see the flippers before it would reach them, you know?

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:24.500
But if both are up at the top, then it would have moved.

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:31.240
You know what I mean? Not really, because that is still what it's doing without two balls, you know?

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:33.820
It feels like that's backwards.

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:41.020
What it should be, unless I'm missing something, what it should be is that it should only track the one that's further from the flippers.

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:46.300
Since you can always see the flippers in the split screen, the one that's closer to the flippers wouldn't need to be tracked.

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:54.900
Well, I mean, I feel like that wouldn't work out because then you don't really have too much lead time at the bottom,

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:58.620
so you would just have a ball fly by, I feel like.

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:00.980
I don't know. But that's what happens when there's only one ball.

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:04.900
I guess maybe I'm not really understanding what you're saying.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:08.500
It's hard to describe it.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:10.820
Maybe I'm not describing it right.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:20.120
But if the camera is only following the ball that is lower, and the split screen shows the lower part of the screen,

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:25.900
it's like there's two things that are benefiting the lower ball and nothing that's paying any attention to the higher ball.

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:32.160
Like, that's why I feel like the camera should be following the ball that's higher if you're going to have the split screen that shows the lower portion of the screen.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:40.720
I think the whole split screen thing is just like so that you always kind of have a handle of what you're actually doing.

00:13:40.720 --> 00:13:44.180
Because you don't have direct control over anything up top.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:53.020
So I guess it would just feel strange to, you know, you're pressing buttons, you're technically doing something, but it's not being shown on screen.

00:13:53.020 --> 00:14:00.820
I mean, I feel like this was sort of a, this was always going to be a design problem when they decided to make something that,

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:03.300
oh, I zoomed out too far, now it looks like crap.

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:10.460
Like, they had to find a way to make this look good and also be able to display like actual information.

00:14:11.140 --> 00:14:19.240
And they can't just have it locked to the bottom because that would then just, you just don't see anything up top.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:23.380
You may as well only have half the, um, half the pinball game.

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:29.120
Um, and then, but you're also not like the, the scoreboard is there.

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:33.420
Um, you're like, whatever you're actually inputting is there.

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:36.960
I think it was the lesser of two evils from a design perspective.

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:38.720
I mean, and I can see that too.

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:43.940
I agree with that, but I guess I just think that they chose to follow the wrong ball with the camera.

00:14:44.420 --> 00:14:48.480
What's funny is it's like, no matter where you net out on this conversation between the two of you,

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:50.380
which has definitely overstayed its welcome.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:52.260
Oh, okay.

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:54.660
Well, yeah, we haven't heard from Mike in long enough.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:55.860
You know, the people want to hear from Mike.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:58.940
No, about whether, which ball it should be tracking.

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:01.180
The real question is why do it at all?

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:03.460
Did it ever bother you with other NES games?

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:04.680
Well, that's the question I was trying to answer.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.940
Right, right. I'm just saying like, think about it.

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:15.100
Other NES games were able to handle this without needing this weird, all of a sudden black bar line that says like,

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:18.900
oh, just so you know, like the, the screen isn't like folding in on itself.

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:22.680
Like the flippers are still down here and you're just looking at what's happening up there.

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:26.760
And then all of a sudden it just locks perfectly back into the same view.

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:27.740
And you're like, oh, right.

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:33.020
Even though I saw my ball falling back down, looking like it was about to approach my flippers.

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:35.520
My flippers aren't at the bottom of the screen yet.

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:39.200
Like they need to move down appropriately to lock back into place and then I can hit them.

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:43.420
It almost makes timing more questionable than if they were just stuck down there.

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:54.440
Yeah. At first it was, it was a little like jarring to me, even though I am like impressed because you just don't see split screen anything on the NES very often.

00:15:54.440 --> 00:16:00.940
So like that was kind of, I guess just, it was cool that they were able to do that, but I don't know.

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:03.800
I don't think I ever had a problem in other pinball games.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:07.540
And once I got used to this, it really wasn't a big deal, but it was jarring at first.

00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:16.720
I think I prefer this to the alternative of just always tracking the ball because I don't know, you're, you don't even really notice it after you're playing for a bit.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:18.960
And I didn't think about that, Joe, but you're right.

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:24.700
There is, there is a novelty to the, to the split screen effort here.

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:27.100
And just like thinking about how does that even work?

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:28.280
Like, how are they doing that?

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:35.100
Like, cause you know, there's, there's only so many sprites that are allowed on a screen and like, how are they, how are they tricking the system?

00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:36.520
So I appreciate that.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:47.280
All right. So, you know, the, the actual game, if you will, is that you're not like trying to disable the pin bot or something like that.

00:16:47.320 --> 00:16:49.020
Like this isn't a fight against a robot.

00:16:49.560 --> 00:17:00.940
The player starts basically traveling the solar system and you start on Pluto and try to hit enough targets and do enough things to continue to move around the solar system to get to the sun.

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:04.480
And you're awarded bonuses for doing those kinds of things.

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:11.440
Uh, if you get to like Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, or the sun, you get like an extra ball, which is kind of cool.

00:17:12.340 --> 00:17:31.220
There's, um, a five by five grid, uh, that's supposed to be like pin bots visor and, um, hitting those kinds of things and, uh, hitting the visor requires like, it basically like does other things that open up other layers and levels to the game.

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:44.860
So there's, there's a lot more going on here than just like, as Sean was saying earlier, here's two bumpers, three targets, um, a bunch more bumpers up top that score some points and one singular ramp.

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:51.040
There's actually like things that you can do and selective bonuses that will show up as you continue to play.

00:17:51.040 --> 00:18:04.340
So rather than break down each thing you can do, I guess I just want to know, did it hold your attention more because there was an ever changing set of like, now you're on this planet?

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:09.560
Like, you know, was that information first conveyed to you in a way that felt appropriate?

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:19.340
And was it more intriguing than say the rock and roll, uh, fifties aesthetic that we saw in, in our other pinball game?

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:22.120
I think that this, this made it more engaging.

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:30.220
Um, I think with as much as I do like these pinball games, like they, they really couldn't hold my attention for that long.

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:34.180
They, they were just kind of, uh, five minutes here or there.

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:39.200
Um, if I do well enough, I'll, I'll play for as long as this ball lasts.

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:48.820
But this one, I actually played for a while because there actually were like objectives and like, you're actually aiming for things that are more than just like points.

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:51.780
Like, it's almost like, what will the, what will the machine do next?

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:54.060
Because things do change over time.

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:57.700
They change more than a pinball machine actually would in real life sometimes.

00:18:57.700 --> 00:19:06.040
But, um, like there's just the different modes, like, okay, now the, now this like wall has opened up and this is where I can lock balls.

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:08.520
And then you get the multi-ball this way.

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:17.440
Like, it was always like something new that you could do if you had like the patience or the, um, uh, or the skill to hit.

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:18.660
Yeah, I agree.

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:22.640
I was always kind of like, I was always kind of checking out what's going to come next.

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:32.740
And, and it left me a little torn on, on, you know, a lot of times I, I talk about on pinball episodes, which is weird that we've had so many of them by now.

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:39.600
But I talk about how, like, why isn't it doing something video gamey that a pinball machine couldn't do?

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:41.160
And this, this does some of that.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:48.240
But before even getting into that, like, there are a lot of things that are changing in here that I'm like, oh, that's really cool.

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:51.600
Because I'm imagining how a real pinball machine must be doing this.

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:56.340
Like, oh, they're like, you know, like they're unlocking this part for you now, or the color scheme is changing.

00:19:56.540 --> 00:20:00.600
But it's not just changing completely, like, because it's a video game and it's able to just do that.

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:06.880
It's like, it's in a lot of cases simulating what I imagine the actual pinball machine does.

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:12.200
And part of that actually just kind of makes me sad that I'm not actually just playing that, that, that machine.

00:20:12.660 --> 00:20:14.300
Because, like, that's really cool.

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:16.260
The analogness of it is really cool.

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:20.940
But then, on the other hand, I do appreciate that it, it did some things.

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:26.560
Like, it gave me a reason to play the video game version rather than play the, the pinball machine.

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:31.200
And, like, of course, the obvious reason is because not everybody has this pinball machine in their living room.

00:20:31.720 --> 00:20:36.760
But, uh, but it, you know, there are some times where I'm like, okay, they did some video gamey things.

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:44.340
And there are other times where I'm like, it's doing really cool things that are clearly from the original game.

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:48.180
And it kind of bums me out that I don't get to see those, you know?

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:50.240
So, like, I don't know. I guess, I don't know exactly what I'm saying.

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:51.400
I guess I'm saying, like, I...

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:53.520
It's an imperfect simulation, right?

00:20:53.860 --> 00:21:00.340
Yeah. And the things that I'm impressed with, I'm, I'm, a lot of the stuff is I'm impressed with the actual pinball machine.

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:06.220
But I'm not sure that, like, I still might be of the opinion that, like, if you're going to give me a video game version of it, just go crazy.

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:08.240
But that's just me.

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:16.700
Yeah, on the same, like, not the same thing, but on the same vibe, like, this is the first time that I'm seeing...

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:25.840
I guess only because this is one of the first games that it's trying to actually simulate physics to, like, this kind of degree.

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:31.440
Um, but, like, the first time that video game physics have...

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:32.260
are wonky.

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:34.440
Because it's obviously...

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:37.580
it's got the actual pinbot machine.

00:21:38.020 --> 00:21:42.040
And you know how a pinball machine should work in real life.

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:44.860
And then just the way, like, it gets caught on things.

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:48.240
And sometimes it feels like the ball clips where it shouldn't.

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:54.440
And those are very forgiving things in, like, a made-for-video game pinball machine.

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:56.980
Because it's, like, usually, like, a flat plane.

00:21:57.260 --> 00:21:59.640
And it's not based on anything.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:04.900
But because this is based on something, uh, like, the ball getting stuck in certain areas

00:22:04.900 --> 00:22:09.400
and just, like, not behaving correctly actually stands out, unlike most NES games.

00:22:09.560 --> 00:22:10.720
So that was also weird.

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:18.700
Yeah, you guys are giving me a lot I want to unpack.

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:24.480
Uh, so I guess I'm gonna just try to go one at a time and see if we can, uh, all stay on the same track here.

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:30.500
First thing that was brought up was a question of mine that, uh, I'm not sure if it was answered or not.

00:22:30.660 --> 00:22:32.460
Don't have to give a whole, like, spiel or anything.

00:22:32.460 --> 00:22:39.320
But I guess I just want to know, were the objectives and the progression clear to you guys?

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:44.380
Or was it still just, like, this is pinball and I'm just gonna keep hitting this ball into things.

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:51.860
Like, I don't understand why I'm getting the multi-ball or why I moved to another level of the machine.

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:56.360
Um, or why my plan, why I'm moving from planet to planet.

00:22:56.500 --> 00:22:58.660
Like, were those objectives clear to you guys?

00:22:59.300 --> 00:23:05.100
Yeah, I was saying that it was clear that there were objectives and I appreciate that there were actually objectives.

00:23:05.660 --> 00:23:07.700
Like, as opposed to the previous games we played.

00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:11.880
Yeah, I'd say some of them became clear to me over time.

00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:14.600
Um, like, as I'm testing things out.

00:23:14.780 --> 00:23:17.060
Or it's like, yeah, there's a giant arrow pointing, like, go here!

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:20.840
You know, and there's, like, things like that where I'm like, oh, can I knock all of these things down and stuff?

00:23:20.900 --> 00:23:23.240
But it's really me trial and error.

00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:25.740
But that's how I am with pinball always.

00:23:25.740 --> 00:23:27.020
I never have any idea what's going on.

00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:30.960
So, so, relatively, yes, it was clear to me.

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:33.840
Yeah, and this is, you know, with pinball, it is a learned thing.

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:38.220
There isn't, um, I guess just like any video game, too.

00:23:38.420 --> 00:23:41.000
Like, it could use a tutorial, in a sense.

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:47.940
And pinball arcade and some other pinball effects and stuff like that have actually worked those into their video versions.

00:23:47.940 --> 00:23:52.820
Where, like, you can actually learn about what you should be doing on the machine before you ever play it.

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:54.400
Which is probably a good idea.

00:23:54.400 --> 00:23:56.100
And I'm sure the manual covered it, too.

00:23:56.220 --> 00:23:59.400
But it's not so complicated here as in newer machines.

00:24:00.020 --> 00:24:04.020
Sean, you followed up on a great topic of the physics of the machine.

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:09.300
Because, you know, pinball physics do matter in terms of realism and everything.

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:16.780
And I feel like even the example we've been using on the PS3, the pinball arcade, is much more forgiving than real-life pinball.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:21.180
And probably why you and I bothered to stay on it for longer than just a single day.

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:25.560
Because it did make us better pinball players than playing on actual machines.

00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:30.900
Where we're just giving up quarters to the loose ball that just doesn't go our way.

00:24:30.980 --> 00:24:32.360
And there's nothing we could have done about it.

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:40.520
But here it did feel, as you were suggesting, a little more forced or fake than usual.

00:24:40.860 --> 00:24:46.400
Where, like, sometimes I even felt like, you know, the ramps were acting almost like vacuums.

00:24:46.540 --> 00:24:50.840
That just, like, there's no way that my shot had enough pull to get around.

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:52.420
But it just did it anyway.

00:24:52.780 --> 00:24:57.420
Or, like, yeah, I totally should have gone down the gutter ramp.

00:24:57.580 --> 00:25:01.680
But instead, it just, like, lucks out and puts it back into the flipper zone.

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:04.980
Like, you know, some of these were kind of bladed.

00:25:05.400 --> 00:25:10.480
Yeah, like, when you're trying to give the ball a little break when it's coming through the chute.

00:25:11.060 --> 00:25:14.760
I don't know if there's a pro pinballer term for that.

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:20.060
Usually when I do that on a real machine, it's, like, hit or miss.

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:21.960
Whether I actually do the intended thing.

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:27.280
And sometimes it just ramps off my paddle and goes where I don't want it to be.

00:25:27.420 --> 00:25:32.800
But when I did it here, it was, like, a perfect, like, oh, that's just going to stop it right where I need it.

00:25:32.840 --> 00:25:34.060
It's going to settle the ball down.

00:25:34.140 --> 00:25:36.640
And then I can do, like, a more precise shot with it.

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:39.160
And I feel like that was too perfect.

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:48.760
I think I'm kind of, like, still just impressed, you know, to, like, have any, like, super positive or negative.

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:52.640
I guess I don't know enough about, like, real pinball.

00:25:52.780 --> 00:25:55.680
I don't play enough of it that, like, really the physics stood out to me.

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:57.680
Well, you bring up a good point there, Joe.

00:25:57.940 --> 00:25:59.680
Like, it is a video game form.

00:25:59.680 --> 00:26:00.800
So does it really matter?

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:10.760
Like, is the goal to be faithful to the pinbot arcade version or is the goal to make it, you know, more video gamey and fun?

00:26:10.880 --> 00:26:13.780
In which case, I do think they succeeded in the goal.

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:19.120
Like, you know, it didn't make me – I wasn't so bothered that the physics weren't accurate that I was like, I can't play this.

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:20.820
It wasn't, you know, it wasn't like that.

00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:23.700
Instead, it was just like, huh, this works differently.

00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:30.460
I think the biggest example of, huh, this works differently is that one of the first tricks – I don't pretend to be an expert at pinball machines.

00:26:30.460 --> 00:26:32.180
I suck at real pinball machines.

00:26:32.180 --> 00:26:33.080
I just know that.

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:42.760
But one of the first tricks you learn if you want to get better at pinball is to keep your flippers up if the ball is coming down, you know, slow enough.

00:26:42.760 --> 00:26:46.580
So this way you can lock the ball and decide when you want to, like –

00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:47.980
That's what I was trying to describe, yeah.

00:26:48.120 --> 00:26:52.560
Right. Let your flipper, like, you know, this way you can roll the ball down and hit it at the exact angle.

00:26:52.560 --> 00:27:02.440
And this game doesn't really give a crap about where you choose to, like, launch that ball on that flipper.

00:27:02.600 --> 00:27:08.780
And I don't mean that in the sense of, like, if you launch it at the very end, it just will still go to the opposite direction.

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:09.960
Like, of course it will.

00:27:10.060 --> 00:27:16.440
But it doesn't really, like, have an exact spot where, like, yeah, hit it in this exact spot and you'll get the ramp.

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:18.760
Like, that never happens in this game.

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:24.060
And so that is, like, an oddball thing of, like, well, in a real pinball machine, like, it would totally do that.

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:25.700
But who cares, right?

00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:32.360
To Joe's point, like, it's not trying to be the actual – or you could go play the actual machine somewhere else.

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:36.200
You're playing the video game form where things are a little more loose and fun.

00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:43.560
And so I'm not saying that this game has to be a perfect pinball simulation to be on the essential games list.

00:27:44.280 --> 00:27:46.040
Yeah, but I don't know.

00:27:46.140 --> 00:27:54.280
I felt, like, the, like, lack of exact precision on, like, something happening exactly the same way every time if you're doing it the same.

00:27:54.320 --> 00:27:59.400
Like, I know that that is literally what would happen, like, in the physical world.

00:28:00.180 --> 00:28:02.540
But I think that – I don't know.

00:28:02.600 --> 00:28:12.000
When I'm playing pinball, it's, like, my brain is not – does not operate quickly enough to, like, actually be exactly the same every time, you know?

00:28:12.280 --> 00:28:20.960
Whereas, like, just because there's so many – you know, an actual pinball has so much, you know, well, I don't know, like, entropy or whatever you'd call it.

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:24.360
But it's, like, too hard for you to actually be exact.

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:40.780
But it feels like maybe on the NES, because it's, like – because it's, like, these pixels, if they were to make it like that, it might be easier to cheese it and to just be, like, oh, yeah, as soon as I see it hit that, like, pixel of the thing, I can hit it and it'll always do exactly the same thing.

00:28:40.880 --> 00:28:46.320
Which I know is literally realistic, but I just feel like from – for human reflexes maybe isn't as realistic.

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:54.140
I think that if they actually tried to do a – there has to be some sort of randomness just in the game.

00:28:54.140 --> 00:29:06.340
Because if it was trying to be as precise as possible and the same input gave the same result, this would probably be a much less fun game to play because you would figure that out.

00:29:06.340 --> 00:29:21.220
And there just isn't enough variation in that entropy as a real pinball machine, which is what gives those – like, there's that combination of kind of chaos, but also if you're really good, you can hit those shots.

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:31.680
I think if they tried to bring that down to a lesser, like, reality, a lesser simulation of reality, it would just be too simple.

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:33.780
Yeah, I totally agree.

00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:52.080
And then for the last point, something Joe was alluding to but rightfully didn't want to jump into it too early into the show, there is a video game format here of, like, taking it further than the actual pinball machine did.

00:29:52.080 --> 00:30:04.220
And I don't want to spoil the Pimbot Arcade machine, but it doesn't have six levels that you can go through in some kind of crazy 3D format.

00:30:04.220 --> 00:30:10.180
Or, like, maybe it would, like, slip off the – like, reveal the next sublayer or something.

00:30:10.320 --> 00:30:12.480
That doesn't happen in the Pimbot machine.

00:30:13.400 --> 00:30:22.700
And, you know, it was smart on Rare, the developer of this game, to say, like, well, we probably could do a little more video game twist to it.

00:30:22.760 --> 00:30:25.700
And so they added the idea of levels to the game.

00:30:25.720 --> 00:30:28.940
And so what does it mean to have another level?

00:30:28.940 --> 00:30:33.020
It's not a completely different pinball map.

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:36.640
It's not, like, the objectives change or whatever.

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:43.240
It's just introducing new challenges and a completely different color scheme to the pinball machine.

00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:56.060
And the way to do this is essentially once you get a multiball, if you're able to score a solar bonus, go around the solar system, you will be able to then enter the next level of the playing field.

00:30:56.380 --> 00:31:03.700
And by level, what happens is, like, in level 1, there are no threats to you other than losing your ball.

00:31:03.700 --> 00:31:09.340
But in level 2, there is now this blob that travels around the solar system.

00:31:09.940 --> 00:31:18.960
And if it gets to the sun, there is a chance that it can eat your ball if you shoot it directly at it, which, spoiler alert, the sun is in the center of the screen.

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:25.320
And so you're at a lot of danger if you shoot anywhere towards the center because most likely it's going to eat your ball.

00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:27.980
So on and so forth for each other level.

00:31:28.100 --> 00:31:32.720
We don't have to get into them, but essentially level 3 adds a plane that flies around and then eventually –

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:33.580
It can blow up your bumper.

00:31:34.200 --> 00:31:35.420
Yeah, they can blow up your –

00:31:35.420 --> 00:31:37.860
That was kind of cool when I first saw that happen.

00:31:37.980 --> 00:31:55.220
Yeah, level – I think level 5 or 4 makes it so that that same plane can now catch your ball too and take it away, which is also kind of terrifying from that standpoint of just something that, like, a shmup enemy that roams around the screen and now your ball is the thing that you don't want to be touched by it.

00:31:55.500 --> 00:32:03.640
All these things keep happening and they're cool, but the game takes it even a step further than that, but maybe not far enough in that, in addition to these,

00:32:03.700 --> 00:32:13.320
changing playfields, there is the ability to change your ball from a ball to a triangle to a cube.

00:32:13.320 --> 00:32:19.660
And yet, well, as cool as that sounds, they didn't make anything different.

00:32:20.080 --> 00:32:21.740
Yeah, it feels the same.

00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:22.840
It just looks different.

00:32:22.840 --> 00:32:23.960
It's the same. It just looks different.

00:32:23.960 --> 00:32:26.920
And they definitely did not change the physics on it.

00:32:26.920 --> 00:32:37.720
I would have loved if the triangle, like, hit differently, you know, like, if it hit the corners in a weird way that made it more likely to get a gutter, like, the stakes are on.

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:42.920
But in exchange, everything that you score is now five times higher or something like that.

00:32:43.040 --> 00:32:54.080
Like, that would have made it worth it because, again, a real pinball machine can't just start adding other shapes because it requires a ball to roll around on the screen.

00:32:54.080 --> 00:32:56.880
On a video game, it doesn't matter what it is.

00:32:57.020 --> 00:32:59.820
You're just telling it to act like a sphere.

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:02.880
It is programmed to work the same way.

00:33:03.040 --> 00:33:13.640
And, you know, coming from, you know, a modern game like Rocket League and seeing that all you got to do is change the shape of, you know, the ball and you have a completely different game.

00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:19.220
That being their version of Rocket-powered car hockey.

00:33:20.100 --> 00:33:22.260
I was expecting a little more out of it.

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:27.580
I mean, obviously not to the same level as Rocket League, but that was a little bit of a disappointment.

00:33:27.580 --> 00:33:38.480
Yeah, it feels really like a missed opportunity for them to decide to change the ball, but only to change it for aesthetic purposes.

00:33:38.900 --> 00:33:41.280
And then to decide from there that, oh, you know what?

00:33:41.360 --> 00:33:45.260
You know what everyone will love in an aesthetic choice is to make it a square.

00:33:46.060 --> 00:33:50.640
Like, if you're going to make it a square, like, I get it if the physics are going to be different.

00:33:50.640 --> 00:33:56.180
But if you just want to, like, oh, what's a cool thing we can change it into, like, you could have picked anything if you're not going to change the physics.

00:33:56.400 --> 00:33:58.460
You're going to pick a triangle, just a shape.

00:33:58.560 --> 00:34:01.760
I think the whole, like, cube thing is just, oh, we're being cheeky.

00:34:02.320 --> 00:34:04.900
Yeah. Use other rare characters.

00:34:05.220 --> 00:34:16.980
There you go. But, you know, all jokes aside on just the ball not changing and everything, I do think, like, you know, we were literally in our last pinball episode, we were asking for this.

00:34:16.980 --> 00:34:19.920
And it's kind of funny that the very next game would deliver on it.

00:34:20.720 --> 00:34:29.600
Does it deliver full way or does it kind of just give you, like, yeah, I mean, that's definitely, like, something you can only do in a video game, but I don't know if it's enough.

00:34:30.740 --> 00:34:34.900
Well, the ball changing gives me that vibe.

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:39.640
But the other stuff I think is pretty cool, like, having things flying around the screen.

00:34:39.940 --> 00:34:46.500
You know, because when I was first playing, I was trying to imagine, okay, like, this is all stuff that could really happen in a pinball machine.

00:34:46.500 --> 00:34:51.920
But then, like, when I got to, like, the, I guess it's a plane, I thought it was, like, a bug or something.

00:34:52.080 --> 00:34:57.400
But the plane flying around, I was like, oh, what, like, that definitely couldn't happen on a real pinball machine.

00:34:57.480 --> 00:34:58.800
But it makes it different.

00:34:58.980 --> 00:35:00.580
I love it.

00:35:00.880 --> 00:35:05.620
I wonder how people who, like, know this pinball machine felt about it.

00:35:05.620 --> 00:35:11.580
Like, you know, I wonder if purists were like, no, this isn't the experience.

00:35:11.820 --> 00:35:14.000
Kids these days need so much stimulation.

00:35:15.060 --> 00:35:19.220
And that's why I think that, you know, it can't have been hard.

00:35:19.220 --> 00:35:21.700
And I say that about a lot of things that are probably hard.

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:23.660
But I mean it for this one.

00:35:23.780 --> 00:35:29.300
It can't have been hard to just have a sim mode and, like, a remix mode.

00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:33.860
Like, just have a selection at the beginning of when you boot the game.

00:35:34.080 --> 00:35:38.280
Have it be, this is the machine you know and love with NES simulation.

00:35:38.820 --> 00:35:47.680
And this is that machine with, like, subcategories of challenges that you can have.

00:35:47.720 --> 00:35:52.420
I think that that's the only, like, big complaint I have.

00:35:52.880 --> 00:35:57.040
Yeah, I mean, I agree that it can't have been hard because the first level already is that.

00:35:57.180 --> 00:36:02.460
Yeah, exactly. So you can just, like, you can just copy that first level and paste it as its own mode.

00:36:06.260 --> 00:36:12.900
And so now that we've seen, at least on the NES scale of, like, video gamifying pinball machines,

00:36:13.280 --> 00:36:16.080
what's on the wish list now for pinball machines?

00:36:16.120 --> 00:36:20.560
What can we, like, put into fruition for the next pinball game we play?

00:36:21.080 --> 00:36:22.180
They can stop.

00:36:22.420 --> 00:36:23.600
More than one machine? I don't know.

